'''Okami Metropolis''' ({{lang|ja|大紙都}}, ''Ōkami-to'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|metropolitan prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] encompassing the national capital, [[Okami]], and the surrounding urban area on the northwestern coast of the island of [[Harujima]]. With a population of 27,215,456 as of 2026, it is the most populous prefecture in Okaiken by a wide margin, containing approximately 33% of the national population within 0.6% of the national land area. The metropolis administers the 20 [[special wards of Okami|special wards]] of the capital city, 20 additional municipalities in the western suburbs, and the outlying [[Ogasawara Islands]] chain in the Shendan Ocean.

Okami Metropolis was formed in 1973 from the merger of the former [[Okami Prefecture]] ({{lang|ja|大紙府}}, ''Okami-fu'') and [[Okami City]] ({{lang|ja|大紙市}}, ''Okami-shi''), creating a unified metropolitan government to manage the explosive post-war growth of the capital region. The merger produced the most complex administrative entity in Okaiken—a prefecture that simultaneously functions as a city, a county, and a regional government, with the 20 special wards operating as individual municipalities within the metropolitan framework.

The metropolis is the economic, political, and cultural centre of Okaiken. The Greater Okami metropolitan area, which extends into the neighbouring prefectures of [[Yonezawa Prefecture|Yonezawa]], [[Tokuyama Prefecture|Tokuyama]], and [[Akita Prefecture|Akita]], accounts for approximately one-third of the nation's GDP and serves as the headquarters for the majority of Okaiken's largest corporations, financial institutions, and cultural organisations. The {{nihongo|"Okami magnet"|大紙磁石|Okami-jishaku}}—the accelerating concentration of population, capital, and talent in the metropolitan area—is the dominant structural trend in the modern Sanese economy.

== History ==

[[File:Okami Honda Shigetomo.png|thumb|right|Honda Shigetomo reviewing the architectural plans for the new capital, with the early construction of Okami Castle visible in the background.]]

=== Pre-1973: Okami Prefecture and Okami City ===
Prior to the 1973 merger, the Okami region was administered as two separate entities: [[Okami Prefecture]] ({{lang|ja|大紙府}}, ''Okami-fu''), which encompassed the capital city and the western suburbs; and [[Okami City]] ({{lang|ja|大紙市}}, ''Okami-shi''), which administered the urban core. The dual structure created persistent governance problems, as the city and the prefecture frequently clashed over jurisdiction, taxation, and urban planning, and the fragmented administration was unable to coordinate the large-scale infrastructure investments needed to manage the capital's explosive growth.

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period saw the Okami metropolitan area grow from approximately 6 million in 1945 to over 20 million by 1970, overwhelming the capacity of the existing administrative structures. The [[Sanese Industrial Miracle]] concentrated economic opportunity in the capital, and the resulting population influx created severe housing shortages, traffic congestion, and environmental degradation that neither the city nor the prefecture could address alone.

=== The 1973 merger ===
The [[Okami Metropolis Act]] of 1973 dissolved both Okami Prefecture and Okami City and created the unified Okami Metropolis ({{lang|ja|大紙都}}, ''Ōkami-to''), a new administrative entity that combined the functions of a prefecture and a municipality. The 20 wards of the former Okami City were redesignated as [[special wards of Okami|special wards]] ({{lang|ja|特別区}}, ''tokubetsu-ku''), each operating as a semi-autonomous municipality with its own elected assembly and mayor, while the western suburbs and the Ogasawara Islands were organised as conventional municipalities under the metropolitan government.

The merger was controversial. Critics argued that the unified structure would concentrate too much power in the metropolitan governor, while supporters argued that the fragmented administration had been the primary obstacle to effective governance. The first Governor of the unified metropolis, [[Kobayashi Ryokichi]], won election on a platform of integrated urban planning and oversaw the construction of the [[Okami Metro]] expansion, the [[Okami Coastal Expressway]], and the first phase of the land reclamation projects that would create the [[Minato]] waterfront development zone.

[[File:Okami Skyline Tama.png|thumb|right|The hyper-dense skyline of modern Tama Island, which absorbed much of the city's post-war commercial growth.]]

=== Modern era ===
The late 20th and early 21st centuries saw Okami Metropolis consolidate its position as the dominant economic and cultural force in Okaiken. The [[Restructuring Era]] of the 1990s redirected the national economy from heavy industry toward services and technology, a transition that favoured the capital's financial sector and its growing technology industry. The [[Satori AI]] ecosystem, centred on the [[Satori Development Institute]] in Kanda, became the nation's most important technology cluster, and the [[Okami Stock Exchange]]'s dominance of capital markets ensured that the metropolis remained the indispensable gateway for both domestic and foreign investment.

The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011, which restored the Imperial House and the traditional capital nomenclature, was a cultural watershed for the metropolis. The city's name was officially restored from the Republican-era "Shinkyō" to the historic "Okami," and the Imperial Palace was renovated and reopened as the primary residence of the [[Emperor of Okaiken|Emperor]]. The Restoration also accelerated the metropolis's cultural dominance, as the revived imperial institutions and the associated ceremonial traditions drew tourism and investment to the capital.

The [[National Iron Body Initiative]], launched by [[Crown Prince Tomohito]] in 2015, had a transformative effect on the metropolis's economy and culture. The Initiative's headquarters in the [[Tatara Sports Complex]] and its subsidies for athletic recovery facilities catalysed the development of the {{nihongo|'''fitness industrial complex'''|健産業|Ken-sangyō}} in the Tatara ward, and the metropolis became the epicentre of the [[Cult of Iron]] physical fitness movement that has become one of the defining features of modern Sanese identity.

== Geography ==

Okami Metropolis occupies the northwestern coast of [[Harujima]], centred on the historic river delta that was transformed by the [[Honda Shogunate]]'s "Dragon's Claw Division" into the five islands that form the urban core. The metropolis extends from the special wards of the capital city through the western suburbs to the [[Ogasawara Islands]] chain in the Shendan Ocean, approximately 1,000 km south of the Harujima mainland.

The mainland portion of the metropolis is divided into two distinct zones: the densely urbanised special wards, which cover the five historic islands and the surrounding reclaimed land; and the western suburbs, which extend into the foothills of the [[Harujima Alps]] and contain the residential communities, research parks, and satellite cities that house the majority of the metropolitan population.

[[File:Ogasawara Islands Chichijima.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The subtropical coast of Chichijima in the Ogasawara Islands, the most remote part of Okami Metropolis.]]

The [[Ogasawara Islands]] ({{lang|ja|小笠原諸島}}, ''Ogasawara-shotō''), a chain of volcanic islands in the Shendan Ocean, are the most remote and sparsely populated part of the metropolis. The islands have a combined population of approximately 77,000 and are administered as a subprefecture. The [[Chichijima]] (pop. 15,122) and [[Hahajima]] (pop. 9,608) are the two largest islands, with the remaining islands supporting small communities of between 13 and 5,325 residents.

=== Climate ===
The mainland portion of Okami Metropolis is located in the [[wikipedia:humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] zone ([[wikipedia:Köppen climate classification|Köppen]] ''Cfa''), with long, hot and humid summers and mild winters. The western suburbs, which extend into the foothills of the Harujima Alps, experience slightly cooler temperatures and higher rainfall than the coastal special wards. The Ogasawara Islands have a tropical climate with warm temperatures year-round.

