== Economy ==

[[File:Okami Stock Exchange Aoi Ward.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Okami Stock Exchange in the Aoi financial district, the largest in Adonia by market capitalisation.]]

Okami Metropolis is the economic engine of Okaiken, generating approximately one-third of the national GDP and serving as the headquarters for the majority of the nation's largest corporations, financial institutions, and cultural organisations. The metropolis's economic dominance has intensified in the 21st century, as the {{nihongo|"Okami magnet"|大紙磁石|Okami-jishaku}}—the accelerating concentration of population, capital, and talent in the capital region—has drawn young workers from every region of the archipelago even as the rural and regional prefectures experience population decline.

The metropolis's economy is dominated by four sectors: financial services, technology, pop culture and entertainment, and the fitness industrial complex. The [[Okami Stock Exchange]], the largest in Adonia by market capitalisation, anchors the financial sector; the [[Satori AI]] ecosystem, centred on the [[Satori Development Institute]], drives the technology sector; the [[O-Pop]] and gaming industries, centred on the [[Tamashima]] entertainment district, power the pop culture sector; and the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] and the [[Cult of Iron]] movement underpin the fitness industrial complex.

=== Satellite cities ===
The western suburbs house the metropolis's satellite cities and research parks, each developing distinct economic identities within the metropolitan framework:

The suburban ring is anchored by several major secondary cities that specialize in distinct economic functions. [[Shigenobu, Okami Metropolis|Shigenobu]] (pop. 725,833) serves as the primary technology corridor, housing the [[Shigenobu Innovation Park]] alongside major AI startups and semiconductor design houses that have earned the district the moniker {{nihongo|"Silicon Ridge"|シリコン尾根|Shirikon-one}}. [[Tobe, Okami Metropolis|Tobe]] (pop. 726,209) forms the largest residential zone in the western suburbs, established as a master-planned community featuring extensive parks and the [[Tobe Central Hospital]].

Industrial and academic research converges in [[Oka, Okami Metropolis|Oka]] (pop. 538,890), home to the [[University of Okami]] and the {{nihongo|Oka Academic Belt|岡学術回廊|Oka-gakujutsu-kairō}}, which produces high patent yields in precision manufacturing and pharmaceutical engineering. Regional logistics is coordinated through [[Kawauchi, Okami Metropolis|Kawauchi]] (pop. 492,708), whose [[Kawauchi Intermodal Terminal]] and freight rail yards process approximately 40% of metropolitan domestic freight distribution.

Maritime commerce is anchored by [[Mitsu-Minato]] (pop. 1,388,670), the second-largest city in the metropolis, whose waterfront encompasses the [[Port of Okami]] container terminals, deep-water drydocks, and cruise terminals. In the southern suburbs, [[Takahama, Okami Metropolis|Takahama]] (pop. 481,358) supports light manufacturing and the traditional [[Takahama Textile District]], while [[Masaki, Okami Metropolis|Masaki]] (pop. 463,469) integrates residential estates with the athletic facilities of the [[Masaki Sports Park]] and the National Iron Body Initiative.

The [[Yonezawa Industrial Zone]], which extends into neighbouring [[Yonezawa Prefecture]], is the largest manufacturing complex in the capital region, anchored by [[Yonezawa Motors]] (400,000 vehicles annually) and [[Usami Heavy Industries]].

For a detailed treatment of the metropolis's economy, see [[Economy of Okami]] and [[Economy of Okaiken]].
Beyond its financial exchanges, Okami Metropolis serves as the strategic planning and digital command center of Guided Capitalism. The Aoi financial district houses the executive secretariats of the Keiretsu Council and the headquarters of the six major corporate conglomerates, including the Sangoku Group, Aizawa Consortium, Mitoyo Holdings, Okami Financial Group, Yonezawa Motors, and Kizan Systems. The metropolis coordinates national artificial intelligence infrastructure through the centralized operations center of the Satori AI platform, which manages trans-archipelagic rail scheduling and automated port logistics. High-frequency computational trading firms and corporate venture funds collaborate with the Okaiken Development Bank to allocate directed industrial credit, financing capital-intensive subsea and geothermal engineering projects across the outer prefectures.

== Demographics ==

Okami Metropolis is the most densely populated prefecture in Okaiken, with a population density of approximately 22,838 per km²—nearly 50 times the national average. The population of 27,215,456 (2026) represents approximately 33% of the national total, a proportion that has been steadily increasing as the Okami magnet draws internal migrants from the declining regional prefectures.

The metropolis's population growth has been driven almost entirely by internal migration. Between 2015 and 2025, the Okami metropolitan area gained approximately 1.2 million residents through net in-migration, while the combined population of the remaining prefectures declined by over 2.8 million. The migrants are disproportionately young (18–34), university-educated, and employed in the service, technology, and creative sectors—precisely the demographics that the regional prefectures can least afford to lose.

The metropolis's ethnic composition is the most diverse in Okaiken. Approximately 500,000 foreign nationals reside in the metropolis, representing over 6% of the special wards' population. The largest foreign communities are from [[Zong]], [[Dagit]], and [[Yanbaru]], with the historic [[Zong Town]] in the Chuo ward remaining the nation's most vibrant ethnic enclave.

== Transportation ==

[[File:Okami Chuo Station.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Okami Chuo Station, the nation's busiest rail terminal and the primary terminus for the Harujima Shinkansen.]]

Okami Metropolis is the central logistical hub of Okaiken's domestic and international transit networks. The [[Okami Metro]], an expansive rapid transit network comprising 13 lines (12 heavy rapid transit lines and the [[Nankō Port Town Line]] automated guideway transit) and 413 stations across {{cvt|512.0|km|mi|abbr=on}} of route, handles approximately 4.98 million unlinked gate entries (20.31 million cumulative line boardings) daily. Together with the 19-line [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR Harujima) commuter network and 14 private railway operators (22 lines, 402 stations), the metropolitan rail system carries 23.83 million daily passengers. [[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chūō Station]] serves as the nation's busiest rail hub (1,376,201 daily passengers) and the primary metropolitan station for the [[Harujima Shinkansen]].

The [[Okami International Airport]] is the nation's busiest, handling approximately 85 million passengers annually. The [[Port of Okami]], centred on the historic Tatara shipyards, handles approximately 92 million tonnes of cargo annually and is the nation's primary commercial harbour.

For a detailed treatment of the metropolis's transportation infrastructure, see [[Urban transport in Okami]].

== Education ==

[[File:University of Okami Campus.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The main campus of the University of Okami in Oka, the nation's second-ranked university.]]

Okami Metropolis is the centre of the nation's higher education system, with the highest concentration of universities and research institutions in Okaiken. The [[Okami Imperial University]] is the nation's most prestigious institution, and the metropolis's research universities—including the [[Okami Institute of Technology]], the [[Satori Development Institute]], and the [[Okami University of the Arts]]—produce the majority of the nation's advanced graduates in science, engineering, and the creative arts.

The [[University of Okami]], located in the city of [[Oka, Okami Metropolis|Oka]], is the nation's second-ranked university and the largest by enrollment. Founded in 1877 as the Okami Prefectural College, it was elevated to university status in 1923 and has since developed into a comprehensive research institution with particular strengths in engineering, pharmaceutical sciences, and applied physics. The university's proximity to the Oka Industrial Research Institute has created one of the metropolis's most productive innovation corridors, and its graduates are heavily recruited by the precision manufacturing and pharmaceutical firms that dominate the Oka local economy.

Other major institutions in the western suburbs include the [[Mitsu-Minato Maritime University]], the [[Shigenobu Institute of Information Sciences]], and the [[Tobe College of Medicine]].

== Culture ==

Okami Metropolis is the undisputed centre of Sanese popular culture. The metropolis's music, fashion, gaming, and media industries generate an estimated 円1.24 trillion in annual revenue and employ approximately 420,000 workers. The [[PYRAMID30]] music charts, the nation's most influential pop culture barometer, are dominated by Okami-based artists and labels, and the city's fashion districts—particularly the [[Tamashima]] Neon District and the [[Aoi]] luxury corridor—set trends that are followed throughout the archipelago.

The [[Cult of Iron]] physical fitness movement, which has become one of the defining features of modern Sanese identity, is centred on the [[Tatara]] ward of the metropolis. The [[National Iron Body Initiative]]'s headquarters in the [[Tatara Sports Complex]] and the ward's {{nihongo|"Tatara Circuit"|多々羅回廊|Tatara-kairō}} of premium gyms, supplement shops, and recovery spas have made the metropolis the epicentre of the national fitness culture.

For a detailed treatment of the metropolis's cultural institutions, see [[Culture of Okami]].

